# LEVEL 9 → 10 — EXTRACTING READABLE STRINGS FROM BINARY DATA


## LEVEL GOAL
Find the password hidden in `data.txt` among human-readable strings, preceded by several `=` characters.

## DESCRIPTION
`data.txt` is mostly binary data. The `strings` command extracts all human-readable sequences from binary files — a staple tool in reverse engineering and malware analysis.

## APPROACH
Running `cat data.txt` produced mostly garbled output. The correct approach was:

```bash
strings data.txt | grep "=="
```

This filtered down to the relevant lines and exposed the password immediately.

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## KEY TAKEAWAY
`strings` scans for sequences of printable characters (default: 4 or more). It is one of the first tools used when examining an unknown binary, firmware image, or suspicious executable.
